<Weblab2>
  <config><![CDATA[{
  "game_id": 74,
  "created_at": "2026-05-28T18:14:39.000Z",
  "level_num": "custom",
  "user_id": 574,
  "properties": {
    "require_edit_to_continue": true,
    "start_sources": {
      "folders": {
      },
      "files": {
        "cf0981d5-aa15-4f83-b899-3d3de658cc7e": {
          "id": "cf0981d5-aa15-4f83-b899-3d3de658cc7e",
          "name": "index.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <title>Drawing Rectangles</title>\n  <style>\n    body {\n      font-family: sans-serif;\n      display: flex;\n      flex-direction: column;\n      align-items: center;\n      margin-top: 20px;\n    }\n    canvas {\n      border: 2px solid black;\n      background-color: lightgray;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>My First Rectangle!</h1>\n  <canvas id=\"myCanvas\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\"></canvas>\n\n  <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": true
        },
        "60af0e8b-9aba-4830-94c9-0f0509732944": {
          "id": "60af0e8b-9aba-4830-94c9-0f0509732944",
          "name": "script.js",
          "contents": "const canvas = document.getElementById(\"myCanvas\");\nconst ctx = canvas.getContext(\"2d\");\n\n// The canvas is 400 pixels wide and 300 pixels high.\n// (0,0) is the top-left corner.\n// X increases to the right, Y increases downwards.\n\n// Set the color for our rectangle\nctx.fillStyle = \"blue\"; // You can try \"red\", \"green\", \"yellow\", or even \"purple\"!\n\n// Draw a rectangle!\n// The parameters are: (x position, y position, width, height)\nctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 75);\n\n// Challenge:\n// 1. Change the color of the rectangle.\n// 2. Change the x and y values to move the rectangle to a different spot.\n// 3. Change the width and height to make it bigger or smaller.\n//    What happens if width or height is 0?\n//    What happens if x or y is outside the canvas?",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "cf0981d5-aa15-4f83-b899-3d3de658cc7e",
        "60af0e8b-9aba-4830-94c9-0f0509732944"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "Welcome back to your digital canvas! In the last level, you learned how the canvas uses x and y coordinates to figure out where to draw things. Remember, (0,0) is the top-left corner, x goes right, and y goes down.\r\n\r\nNow, let's draw our very first shape: a rectangle! Open up the `script.js` file. You'll see some code that gets your canvas ready for drawing. The key line for us is `ctx.fillRect()`. This special command tells the computer to draw a filled rectangle.\r\n\r\nTake a close look at the numbers inside `ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 75);`. These numbers are very important!\r\n*   The first number (50) is the **x-coordinate** where the rectangle starts.\r\n*   The second number (50) is the **y-coordinate** where the rectangle starts.\r\n*   The third number (100) is the **width** of the rectangle.\r\n*   The fourth number (75) is the **height** of the rectangle.\r\n\r\nYour challenge is to play with these numbers in `script.js`:\r\n1.  Change the color of the rectangle by modifying `ctx.fillStyle`. Try names like 'red', 'green', or 'purple'.\r\n2.  Change the first two numbers in `ctx.fillRect()` to move your rectangle around the canvas.\r\n3.  Change the third and fourth numbers to make your rectangle wider, narrower, taller, or shorter. What happens if you make a width or height 0? Or if the x and y coordinates are really big?",
    "generate_outline": "Teach students how to draw a rectangle using a function like `drawRect(x, y, width, height)`. Students will practice drawing a single rectangle at a specified position and size on the canvas."
  },
